  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This study motivates identifying and characterizing CTB3, a CAMTA-like calmodulin-binding transcription factor and favorable wild-rice-derived CTB3 allele, and developing/evaluating CTB3-targeted breeding approaches to improve booting-stage cold tolerance and cold adaptation in japonica rice, supporting cultivation in colder or high-latitude environments.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) The retained and repaired terminal conclusions jointly motivate the paper-level research proposal."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Together, CTB3 as a calmodulin-binding transcription factor controlling booting-stage cold tolerance and its favorable Chinese wild-rice-derived allele motivate developing and evaluating CTB3-targeted breeding strategies for cold-tolerant japonica rice."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Because rice cold tolerance is polygenic and developmental-stage specific, discovering additional booting-stage cold-tolerance genes is warranted for improving booting-stage cold tolerance."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) The Chinese wild-rice-derived CTB3 allele reported to promote cold adaptation of japonica rice is a plausible CTB3 breeding target to evaluate for improving rice cold tolerance and geographic adaptation."];
  "i11" [label="(0,0,0) If CAMTA TFs mediate abiotic stress and low-temperature responses in Arabidopsis, then a plausible hypothesis is that rice CAMTAs may also regulate cold tolerance (including at reproductive/booting stage)."];
  "i12" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Currently CAMTAs are known stress/low-temperature regulators in other plants, and the phenomenon is that rice CAMTA cold role is unknown; so it is plausible that a rice CAMTA could underlie booting-stage cold tolerance."];
  "i15" [label="(0,0,0) If a gene/allele improves booting-stage cold tolerance and is a selectable natural variant, then it can serve as a target for marker-assisted selection or molecular breeding to improve cold tolerance and expand cultivation to colder regions."];
  "i19" [label="(0,0,0) If a well-motivated booting-stage gene discovery program identifies a CAMTA-like TF gene (CTB3) that controls booting-stage cold tolerance, then CTB3 becomes the core research focus and breeding target."];
  "i20" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently the program is motivated and CTB3 is identified as controlling booting-stage cold tolerance; and if that implies focus/target, then CTB3 is the key target/mechanism to improve booting-stage cold tolerance."];
  "i5" [label="(0,0,0) If booting-stage cold injury directly reduces seed setting and yield with irreparable losses, then improving booting-stage cold tolerance is especially valuable for production."];
  "i6" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently booting-stage cold injury causes sterility and yield loss, and if that implies special value, then booting-stage cold tolerance improvement is especially valuable."];
  "i7" [label="(0,0,0) If cold tolerance is polygenic and stage-specific, then discovering additional stage-specific genes is necessary to improve cold tolerance at a particular stage."];
  "i8" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently cold tolerance is polygenic and stage-specific, and if that implies need for stage-specific discovery, then discovering booting-stage genes is necessary for booting-stage improvement."];
  "n18" [label="(31,0,0) Currently, whether CAMTA family transcription factors play a role in rice cold acclimatization remains unknown."];
  "n19" [label="(32,0,0) Currently (this work reports), CTB3 encodes a calmodulin-binding transcription factor controlling cold tolerance at the booting stage."];
  "n20" [label="(33,0,0) Currently (this work reports), a CTB3 allele with 57-bp and 284-bp deletions from Chinese wild rice promotes cold adaptation of japonica rice."];
  "n7" [label="(7,0,0) Currently, cold injury at the booting stage causes spikelet sterility, reduced seed setting, yield decrease, and irreparable losses."];
  "n9" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, cold tolerance is a complex trait controlled by multiple genes, and different genes act at different developmental stages."];
  "ANS_SRC_1" [label="(35,0,0) Currently, Furthermore, CTB3 activates the expression of OsTPP1, leading to reduced Tre6P content."];
  "ANS_SRC_2" [label="(36,0,0) Currently, The lower Tre6P content promotes the expression of sugar-transporter-related genes, which increase the accumulation of sugar in the panicles, thereby enhancing cold tolerance."];
  "ANS_SRC_3" [label="(34,0,0) Currently, OsTCP19 suppresses CTB3 expression by binding to the two indel regions and thereby negatively regulates cold tolerance at the booting stage."];
  "ANS_SRC_4" [label="(37,0,0) Currently, Our results show that the two favorable alleles of OsTCP19 and CTB3 have been selected and retained in japonica rice to improve cold tolerance and promote the expansion of rice to high-latitude regions."];
